'''Lesbian, gay, bisexual, transgender and queer''' ('''[[w:LGBTQ+|LGBTQ+]]''') '''rights in [[Okaiken]]''' reflect a cultural and legal framework that diverges substantially from both the liberal identity-based models of [[Balisca]] and [[Adonian]] progressive nations, and the more restrictive approaches of neighboring countries. Okaiken has never criminalized same-sex behavior, and the cultural foundations of the {{nihongo|Cult of Iron|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} and the historical {{nihongo|''nenja''|念者|"intimate mentor"}} tradition have long provided a socially legible framework for same-sex bonds between men.

However, Okaiken's approach is not characterized by the Western framework of identity politics. The law addresses '''behavior and relationships''' rather than identity categories. Citizens are not formally classified as gay, bisexual, or transgender under Okaiken law; instead, protections attach to specific acts of discrimination against relationships and gender expressions. This has been praised by some as culturally authentic and criticized by others—particularly those aligned with international LGBTQ+ advocacy networks—as a form of structural erasure.

The most significant recent development has been the legalization of the {{nihongo|Hanryo tōroku|伴侶登録|Companion Registration}} in 2014, a legally equivalent alternative to civil marriage rooted in the historical {{nihongo|''nenja''|念者}} sworn-bond tradition, which carries full inheritance, medical, and property rights. Same-sex civil marriage in the Western sense does not exist in Okaiken, and there is limited domestic appetite for it as a political goal, given that the Companion Registration system is widely regarded as functionally equivalent.

==Historical and cultural context==
===The nenja tradition and the absence of criminalization===
Unlike many nations whose legal systems absorbed Western religious morality during periods of modernization, Okaiken's legal history contains no prohibition on same-sex behavior. The {{nihongo|''nanshoku''|男色|"male love"}} tradition—encompassing same-sex bonds between men across religious, military, and civilian life—was woven into the institutional fabric of the Sengoku and Honda periods. The relationship between [[Anjō Motonobu]] and his protégé [[Murakami Motosuke]], documented in the ''[[Chronicles of the Iron Path]]'' (1632), became the cultural archetype for the {{nihongo|''nenja''|念者}} bond: an intimate sworn companionship combining physical mentorship, emotional fidelity, and, implicitly, erotic admiration.

This tradition was never formally suppressed. The [[Saito Restoration]] weakened institutional {{nihongo|''wakashū''|若衆}} but did not criminalize same-sex behavior, and subsequent governments maintained this position. When the [[Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011 reoriented Okaiken's cultural politics toward Sengoku-era values, it implicitly rehabilitated the {{nihongo|''nenja''|念者}} tradition as a component of national heritage rather than a target of reform.

===The Marebito: a third gender in Shoto tradition===
[[File:Marebito Shrine.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A {{nihongo|''Marebito''|稀人}} shrine attendant at the [[Tesshin Grand Shrine]], [[Okami]]. The ''Marebito'' have historically served as sacred intermediaries at major animist shrines.]]
The {{nihongo|''Marebito''|稀人|"rare/liminal person"}} is a category rooted in [[Shoto]] animist theology. In classical Shoto cosmology, the ''Marebito'' were sacred visitors from beyond ordinary reality—liminal figures who crossed the threshold between the mortal and divine worlds. By the Honda period, the term had acquired a secondary social meaning: it was applied to individuals whose gender presentation or social role placed them outside conventional male-female divisions.

''Marebito'' individuals historically served roles as shrine attendants, oracles, and mediators in animist ritual contexts. Their liminality was understood not as deficiency but as sacred capacity: the ability to stand between categories was interpreted as proximity to the divine. This afforded ''Marebito'' individuals a degree of social protection and prestige unavailable in cultures that pathologized gender non-conformity.

The contemporary usage of ''Marebito'' as a culturally recognized gender category is not legally codified in the same manner as Western non-binary designations. Okaiken's civil registration system operates on a binary sex marker, and there is no formal procedure to change one's registered sex. However, ''Marebito'' individuals are recognized in Shoto shrine contexts, in NIBI's cultural programming, and increasingly in corporate diversity frameworks—a form of cultural legibility that operates beneath the threshold of formal legal recognition.

International LGBTQ+ advocates have criticized this arrangement, noting that the absence of legal gender recognition leaves ''Marebito'' individuals without formal protection in administrative contexts. Domestic advocates have been more divided, with some arguing that the Shoto framework offers a form of dignity that legal categorization alone cannot provide, and others noting the practical vulnerabilities it creates.

===The Cult of Iron and sublimated homoeroticism===
{{main|Cult of Iron|National Iron Body Initiative}}
The {{nihongo|Cult of Iron|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} and its institutional successor, the [[National Iron Body Initiative]] (NIBI), have created a culturally sanctioned space for the admiration and physical intimacy of the male form that functions, in the analysis of scholars such as [[Haruki Sone]], as a form of {{nihongo|"sublimated homoeroticism"|昇華された同性愛|shōka sareta dōseiai}}. Iron Body Centers have become de facto social spaces for men who have relationships with other men, particularly in areas where explicitly designated social venues are absent.

Crucially, this framework does not require or encourage self-identification. A man who trains alongside other men, admires their physical development, and maintains intimate bonds with male companions operates fully within the cultural mainstream of Okaiken masculinity. The gymnasium provides a context in which these behaviors carry no stigma—indeed, carry positive cultural valence—without requiring the practitioner to adopt an identity label.

==Legal framework==
===Behavior and relationship, not identity===
Okaiken's legal approach to what other nations would call LGBTQ+ rights operates through a behavior-and-relationship framework rather than an identity-based one. This distinction is foundational. No Okaiken law uses the terms "homosexual," "bisexual," or their Sanese equivalents as legal categories. Instead:

* Protections against workplace discrimination are framed around the prohibition of adverse treatment based on '''relational status'''—including Companion Registration partnerships—and '''gender expression''', without specifying that the target of protection must identify with any particular identity category.
* The {{nihongo|Hanryo tōroku|伴侶登録|Companion Registration}} system confers rights on the basis of a registered relationship, not the sexual orientation of its parties. Two men, two women, or any combination of individuals may register as companions regardless of how they understand their own identity.
* Shrine-recognized ''Marebito'' status carries informal social protections within Shoto institutional contexts, though these are not enforceable in civil courts.

Critics of this framework—including [[Ren Ishikawa]], a prominent ''Marebito'' activist based in [[Okami]]—have argued that the absence of identity categories creates a structural invisibility. "You can be protected as a companion," Ishikawa wrote in 2026, "but you cannot be protected as yourself. The law sees our relationships; it refuses to see us."

Defenders of the framework, including legal scholar [[Tomoko Fujiwara]] of [[Okami Imperial University]], counter that identity-based law carries its own risks: it creates fixed categories that can become targets, and it imports a Western conceptual architecture that may not map cleanly onto Okaiken social reality.

===The Companion Registration system===
{{main|Hanryo tōroku}}
The {{nihongo|Hanryo tōroku|伴侶登録|Companion Registration}} was established by legislation in 2014, following a decade of advocacy from labor and civil society groups. It is legally equivalent to civil marriage in all material respects:

* Full mutual inheritance rights
* Joint medical decision-making authority
* Shared property registration
* Spousal equivalence for tax and pension purposes
* Parental recognition for jointly adopted children (since 2019)

The Companion Registration draws its name and ceremonial form from the historical {{nihongo|''nenja''|念者}} sworn-bond tradition. Civil registration is conducted at municipal offices, but the culturally preferred ceremony takes place at a [[Shoto]] animist shrine, where the couple presents offerings and receives a formal blessing from a shrine priest. The ceremony, known as the {{nihongo|''Seiyaku no gi''|誓約の儀|"rite of the sworn bond"}}, involves the symbolic exchange of iron tokens—a practice derived from the Honda-period custom of exchanging iron rings as tokens of warrior brotherhood.

The [[Tesshin Grand Shrine]] in [[Okami]] has become the most prominent site for ''Seiyaku no gi'' ceremonies, attracting couples from across the country. The shrine's head priest, [[Akira Nishizawa]], has publicly described the ceremony as a restoration of an interrupted tradition rather than a new institution: "We are not inventing anything. We are remembering."

The Companion Registration system has been criticized from two directions. Conservative religious voices within the [[Confucian Reform Society]] have argued that extending the {{nihongo|''nenja''|念者}} tradition beyond its original warrior-class context dilutes its meaning. International LGBTQ+ advocates have noted that the system's framing around a historically male institution creates friction for female same-sex couples, who lack an equivalent cultural precedent of equal prominence.

===Adoption===
Joint adoption by registered companions has been legal since 2019, following an amendment to the {{nihongo|Family Relations Act|家族関係法|Kazoku kankei-hō}}. The amendment passed with cross-party support, framed primarily as a child welfare measure—expanding the pool of eligible adoptive households—rather than as an LGBTQ+ rights measure, reflecting the government's consistent preference for policy framing that avoids identity-political language.

===Military service===
Okaiken has never maintained a formal policy restricting military service on the basis of sexual behavior or relationship status. The military's longstanding internal culture—shaped by the {{nihongo|''nenja''|念者}} tradition and the Cult of Iron's celebration of male physical bonds—has historically been among the more tolerant institutional environments in Okaiken society. Senior military figures have occasionally appeared at ''Seiyaku no gi'' ceremonies for serving personnel without incident or official comment.

==Social landscape==
===Urban and rural divergence===
Contrary to the pattern observed in many industrialized nations, social openness toward same-sex relationships and ''Marebito'' identity in Okaiken is not straightforwardly correlated with urbanization. Rural and provincial communities, where Iron Body Centers serve as primary social institutions and where the {{nihongo|''nenja''|念者}} tradition retains stronger cultural memory, tend to exhibit a more relaxed and unreflective acceptance of same-sex bonds. The gymnasium's communal intimacy, the shrine's ''Marebito'' tradition, and the close-knit nature of provincial social life create an environment in which same-sex bonds are simply part of the texture of community life.

Urban Okami and the major port cities present a more complex picture. Greater exposure to international cultural currents has introduced Western identity frameworks, which some urban residents find clarifying and others find alienating. A degree of urban conservatism—manifested not as overt hostility but as a cooler, more distanced formality—exists alongside a visible cosmopolitan LGBTQ+ scene centered on neighborhoods such as [[Takehara-chō]] in central Okami. The tension between these currents is most visible in generational terms: younger urban Okaiken residents are more likely to use imported identity terminology, while older residents across all regions tend toward the behavior-and-relationship framework.

===The bishōnen rejection and class stigma===
A distinctive feature of Okaiken's social landscape is the widespread cultural rejection of the {{nihongo|''bishōnen''|美少年|"beautiful boy"}} aesthetic—the slender, androgynous male ideal associated with idol entertainment culture. This rejection is not primarily driven by hostility to same-sex attraction; men who are attracted to other men are broadly socially legible within the Cult of Iron framework. Rather, the {{nihongo|''bishōnen''|美少年}} aesthetic is rejected on '''class and cultural grounds''': it is associated with passivity, commercial exploitation, and the aestheticization of weakness.

In popular cultural discourse, {{nihongo|''bishōnen''|美少年}} presentation is coded as {{nihongo|"lesser class"|下等|Katō}}—a term that carries connotations of inauthenticity and susceptibility to manipulation. The entertainment industry's historical exploitation of young male performers, which the [[Youth Entertainment Regulation Act]] was designed to address, has attached a moral taint to the aesthetic itself. Men who present in a {{nihongo|''bishōnen''|美少年}} manner face social friction not primarily because of assumed same-sex attraction but because their presentation is read as either a marker of exploitation or an affectation borrowed from a discredited cultural form.

This creates a paradox noted by several scholars: Okaiken is broadly tolerant of same-sex bonds between physically imposing men who conform to the {{nihongo|"guardian physique"|守護者体格|shugoshitaikaku}} ideal, while simultaneously stigmatizing a gender presentation that might, in other cultural contexts, be associated with gay male identity. The acceptance is real but conditional on a specific performance of masculinity.

===The labor movement and civil society===
The primary organizational vehicle for what might in other contexts be called LGBTQ+ advocacy in Okaiken is the {{nihongo|Companion Rights Federation|伴侶権連合|Hanryo-ken Rengō}}, a coalition of labor unions, civil society organizations, and shrine associations. The Federation does not use LGBTQ+ identity language in its official materials, preferring the framing of "relationship rights" and "expression protections."

The Federation's origins lie in the backlash to the [[Youth Entertainment Regulation Act]] of the mid-2000s. While the Act was primarily framed as child protection legislation, its enforcement disproportionately targeted entertainment industries associated with the {{nihongo|''bishōnen''|美少年}} aesthetic, and its implementation exposed a broader pattern of discrimination against workers whose gender expression or relationship status fell outside conventional norms. A series of workplace discrimination cases in the early 2010s—most notably the {{nihongo|Kuramoto Case|倉本事件|Kuramoto Jiken}} of 2012, in which a shrine attendant known to be in a same-sex partnership was dismissed from a corporate position following the disclosure of his relationship status—galvanized the Federation's founding coalition.

The Federation's primary legislative achievements have been:

* The 2013 {{nihongo|Workplace Expression Protection Guidelines|職場表現保護指針|Shokuba hyōgen hogo shishin}}, prohibiting adverse employment actions based on gender expression or relationship status in public institutions and NIBI-registered facilities
* The 2014 Companion Registration legislation
* The 2019 joint adoption amendment

Current Federation priorities include extending workplace protections to the private sector comprehensively, establishing civil enforcement mechanisms for ''Marebito'' discrimination in administrative contexts, and securing formal legal recognition of the ''Seiyaku no gi'' ceremony's civil effects without requiring separate municipal registration.

==Controversies and criticisms==
===International advocacy tensions===
Okaiken's relationship with international LGBTQ+ advocacy organizations has been complicated. International bodies have praised the Companion Registration system and the absence of criminalization, while criticizing the lack of identity-based legal protections, the absence of formal gender recognition procedures, and the cultural stigma attached to {{nihongo|''bishōnen''|美少年}} presentation. Several international reports have characterized Okaiken's framework as "tolerant but not affirming"—a formulation that Okaiken advocates have contested as importing cultural assumptions that do not translate.

[[Ren Ishikawa]], whose 2026 essay {{nihongo|"The Iron Nation and Its Conditions"|鉄の国家とその条件|Tetsu no kokka to sono jōken}} attracted significant international attention, articulated the tension with characteristic precision: "Okaiken will embrace you if you are strong. It will celebrate your bond if it resembles the bonds of warriors. It will call you ''Marebito'' and grant you shrine space if your liminality serves its rituals. What it will not do is simply see you, unconditioned, as yourself."

===The Nakayama legacy===
[[Rento Nakayama]]'s tenure as Minister of Health and Welfare introduced a populist framing of physical wellness that, some advocates argue, implicitly reinforced the conditional nature of Okaiken's tolerance. By centering the {{nihongo|"guardian physique"|守護者体格|shugoshitaikaku}} as the national masculine ideal, the [[National Iron Body Initiative]] under Nakayama's stewardship created a framework in which same-sex bonds were celebrated precisely insofar as they conformed to a specific physical and behavioral type. Nakayama himself never addressed LGBTQ+ rights directly during his tenure, a silence that Federation representatives characterized as "calculated ambiguity."

===Conservative opposition===
The [[Conservative Reform Society]] and elements of the conservative press have opposed the Companion Registration system on the grounds that it extends the sacred {{nihongo|''nenja''|念者}} tradition beyond its original warrior-class and religious contexts. The journal ''National Essence'' argued in 2015 that "the ''Seiyaku no gi'' belongs to the dojo and the shrine, not the municipal registry office. To bureaucratize it is to hollow it out."

A smaller current of conservative opinion has objected to the Federation's labor-movement framing, arguing that embedding relationship rights within labor advocacy corrupts both causes. This position has found little mainstream political traction.

